A(('p', '3.11 What the account leaves unsaid. It does not say who the nations are, who “my brethren” are, or who “the least of these” are. It does not say when or where the throne stands, what “the kingdom prepared for you” is beyond those words, or what “everlasting fire” and “everlasting punishment” are beyond those words.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 What the King says. Matthew does not give an explanation after the account, because the account is itself the King’s speech. Within it the King states what He says about what was done and not done. He says to the right hand: “Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me” (25:40). He says to the left hand: “Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as ye did it not to one of the least of these, ye did it not to me” (25:45). Everything the King identifies is in these two verses, and the table records it in His words.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of what the King says', 'What the King says', 'Verse'],
    ['How He introduces it', '“Verily I say unto you”', 'Matt 25:40, 25:45'],
    ['What was done', '“Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren”', 'Matt 25:40'],
    ['To whom, He says, it was done', '“ye have done it unto me”', 'Matt 25:40'],
    ['What was not done', '“Inasmuch as ye did it not to one of the least of these”', 'Matt 25:45'],
    ['To whom, He says, it was not done', '“ye did it not to me”', 'Matt 25:45'],
], [0.30, 0.50, 0.20]))
A(('p', '4.2 The close. The account ends with a sentence about both companies: “And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal” (25:46). It names “everlasting punishment” and “life eternal.” These are the words given. They are not expanded here.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of the close', 'What the account says', 'Verse'],
    ['Those who go away', '“these shall go away”', 'Matt 25:46'],
    ['Where they go', '“into everlasting punishment”', 'Matt 25:46'],
    ['Who goes the other way', '“but the righteous”', 'Matt 25:46'],
    ['Where they go', '“into life eternal”', 'Matt 25:46'],
], [0.30, 0.50, 0.20]))
A(('p', '4.3 What the King does not name. The King names what was done and not done and says it was done or not done “unto me.” He leaves the other parts of the account without an identification. Jesus does not identify the nations. Jesus does not identify the sheep or the goats beyond the words “the righteous” and “ye cursed.” Jesus does not identify “my brethren.” Jesus does not identify “the least of these.” Jesus does not state in so many words that “one of the least of these my brethren” in 25:40 and “one of the least of these” in 25:45 name the same persons, although the second omits “my brethren.” Jesus does not state what “the kingdom prepared for you” is beyond those words. Jesus does not state what “everlasting fire” or “everlasting punishment” is beyond those words, and He does not state what “life eternal” is beyond those words. Jesus does not identify “the devil and his angels” beyond the words of 25:41.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because the King names only what was done and not done, and says it was done or not done unto Him, nothing further is named here. A reader who says that the nations are a certain people, that “my brethren” are a certain company, that “the least of these” are certain persons, or that the fire or the life is a certain place or condition, is supplying what the King did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 The explanation stays with its account. The King’s words in Matthew 25:40 and 25:45 belong to this account. The shared word “least” and the shared word “brethren” elsewhere do not make other sayings one with these. “Take heed that ye despise not one of these little ones” (Matthew 18:10) and “whosoever shall give to drink unto one of these little ones a cup of cold water only in the name of a disciple” (10:42) are Jesus’ words in other settings, and Jesus does not join them to the words of 25:40.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparison and the account themselves. It repeats none of the King’s identifications from Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the account.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. The one comparison in the account is “as a shepherd divideth his sheep from the goats” (25:32). A shepherd has both in one flock, and he separates them. The meaning of the comparison is in the movement: a gathering, a dividing and a setting on the right hand and on the left.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the account communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The separation is made by the King.** The Son of man comes in his glory, sits on the throne of his glory and separates. The account does not have the nations separate themselves.',
    '**The separation is complete.** The nations are gathered, the separation is “one from another,” and each company is set on one side.',
    '**What was done is counted.** The King names six kinds of need and says whether each was met. The account treats the meeting of need, or the failure to meet it, as something He weighs.',
    '**It is counted as done to Him.** The King says what was done to one of the least of these was done to Him, and the same for what was not done.',
    '**Neither side foresaw it.** Both the righteous and those on the left ask “when saw we thee.” The account shows that neither company said they had seen Him in those who were in need.',
    '**There are two ends.** The account closes on “everlasting punishment” and “life eternal.”',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers were the disciples who asked about His coming and the end (24:3). They were meant to understand, from the account, that when the Son of man comes in His glory, a separation of the nations is made by the King and that what has been done to the least of these, or not done, is reckoned as done or not done to Him. These are statements about that coming, and they are not about the church of the Body.'))
A(('p', '5.4 What this section does not do. It does not say who the nations are, or who “my brethren” or “the least of these” are. It does not say whether the account describes one judgment or one day. It does not say that a person is saved or lost by meeting the needs the King names. It does not turn feeding, drinking, welcome, clothing, visiting or coming unto into a way of being saved. It draws no meaning from the ten virgins, the talents or any other parable into this account.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The sheep and the goats stands at the end of Matthew 25. It is kept apart here from four neighbors.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 25:1–13', '“Then shall the kingdom of heaven be likened unto ten virgins”', 'The Ten Virgins. A story of lamps, oil and a bridegroom, which has its own study. It is not read into this account and this account is not read into it.'],
    ['Matt 25:14–30', '“a man travelling into a far country, who called his own servants”', 'The Talents. A story of servants and goods, which has its own study. Its close in 25:30 is not carried into 25:31–46.'],
    ['Matt 24:45–51', '“Who then is a faithful and wise servant”', 'The Faithful and Unfaithful Servant. A story of a servant over a household, with its own setting and its own study.'],
    ['Matt 13:24–30', '“The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed”', 'The Wheat and the Weeds. A story of a field and a harvest, with its own study. Its words are not used to explain the separation here.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The ten virgins and the talents are told in the discourse of chapters 24 and 25 as stories introduced with “the kingdom of heaven” and “a man.” The sheep and the goats opens with “When the Son of man shall come in his glory” (25:31). The wheat and the weeds is told in Matthew 13, in another discourse. Each is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The sheep and the goats has the Son of man, the King, the holy angels, the nations, the righteous, those on the left hand and the devil and his angels named in 25:41. The ten virgins have virgins and a bridegroom, the talents have servants and a lord, and the wheat and the weeds have a sower, servants and an enemy. No character is carried from one account to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The sheep and the goats moves from a coming to a gathering, a separation, words to each side and a close. The ten virgins move from a waiting to a shut door, and the talents from a trust to a reckoning. None of those movements is read into this account.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, “everlasting fire” in 25:41 is not “outer darkness” in 25:30, and neither is used to explain the other. Second, “the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world” in 25:34 is not read through any other use of that phrase. Third, “my brethren” in 25:40 is not read through the use of “brethren” in other passages, and Jesus does not join them.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks to the disciples who came to Him privately on the mount of Olives (Matthew 24:3), and Matthew says He spoke these sayings “unto his disciples” (26:1). It belongs to the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matthew 4:17).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The sheep and the goats was spoken before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in Matthew 25:31–46 speaks of the Body of Christ, and the passage is not read as though the Body were already formed. The Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul is read in Paul’s letters.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The gathering, the separation, the throne and the six needs are not a description of the Body of Christ. The account is not read as a universal picture of how any person is judged. Feeding, giving drink, welcoming, clothing, visiting and coming unto are actions in the King’s words, and they are not a mechanism of salvation. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this account: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9). The kingdom prepared for the sheep and the fire prepared for the devil and his angels are not the Body’s promise or the Body’s destiny.'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the account shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of attention.** The King names hunger, thirst, a stranger, nakedness, sickness and prison. A reader learns to notice such need in those near him.',
    '**A lesson of action.** The matter is what was done or not done. A reader learns that seeing need and doing nothing is a failure to act.',
    '**A lesson of unlooked-for service.** Neither company asked “when saw we thee” expecting to be asked. A reader learns to serve without waiting to be sure that anyone is watching.',
    '**A lesson of weighing consequence.** The account closes on two ends. A reader learns to treat the weight Jesus puts on the way people are treated as real.',
    '**A lesson of care for the overlooked.** The King speaks of “one of the least of these.” A reader learns to count the one who is easily overlooked as someone to whom care is owed.',
]))
A(('p', 'None of these lessons says that the Body is the sheep, that the Body is the nations, or that the Body is “my brethren.” They are principles drawn from what the account shows.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'The Son of man comes in his glory with all the holy angels and sits on the throne of his glory. All nations are gathered before him, and he separates them one from another, as a shepherd divides his sheep from the goats, setting the sheep on his right hand and the goats on the left. The King tells the right hand to come and inherit the kingdom prepared for them from the foundation of the world, because they fed, gave drink, took in, clothed and visited. He tells the left hand to depart into everlasting fire prepared for the devil and his angels, because they did none of these. Each company asks when they saw him in need, and the King answers that what was done or not done to one of the least of these was done or not done to him (Matthew 25:40, 45). The account closes on everlasting punishment and life eternal (25:46). The King names nothing else. He spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the nations, the brethren or the least of these are, or that makes feeding, visiting or clothing a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a coming in glory, a gathering of all nations, a separation, words to the right hand, words to the left hand, and a close.',
    '**The meaning:** the King separates the nations, and He counts what was done or not done to one of the least of these as done or not done to Him.',
    '**The boundaries:** only what was done and not done, and “unto me,” are explained by the King; the nations, the brethren, the least of these, the fire and the life are not.',
    '**The present lesson:** attention, action, unlooked-for service, weighed consequence and care, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

